keeping. If you are this author and want this page removed, please let us know.\n\n## Publications (91)\n\nLeast Consecutive Pair of Quadratic Nonresidues\n\nPreprint\n\nJune 2026\n\n·\n\n4 Reads\n\nN. A. Carella\n\nLet $p>1$ be a large prime number and let $x=O((\\log p)^2(\\log\\log p)^3$ be a real number. It is proved that the least consecutive pair of quadratic nonresidues $u\\ne\\pm1, v^2$ and u+1 satisfies the upper bound $u\\ll x$ in the prime field $\\mathbb{F}_p$.\n\n[Button: Read more]\n\nLeast Consecutive Pair of Primitive Roots\n\nPreprint\n\nApril 2026\n\n·\n\n6 Reads\n\nN. A. Carella\n\nLet $p>1$ be a large prime number and let $x=O((\\log p)^2(\\log\\log p)^5$ be a real number. \nPreprint\n\nSeptember 2024\n\n·\n\n9 Reads\n\nN. A. Carella\n\nThis note determines an effective asymptotic formula for the number of squarefree totients $p-1$ with a fixed primitive root $u\\ne \\pm 1, v^2$.\n\n[Button: Read more]\n\nSmall Prime kth Power Residues and Nonresidues in Arithmetic Progressions\n\nPreprint\n\nMay 2024\n\n·\n\n10 Reads\n\nN. A. Carella\n\nLet p be a large odd prime, let $x=(\\log p)^{1+\\varepsilon}$ and let $q\\ll\\log\\log p$ be an integer, where $\\varepsilon>0$ is a small number. This note proves the existence of small prime quadratic residues and prime quadratic nonresidues in the arithmetic progression $a+qm\\ll x$, with relatively prime $1\\leq a<q$, unconditionally. The same results are generalized to small prime k th power residues and nonresidues, where $k\\mid p-1$ and $k\\ll\\log\\log p$.\n\n[Button: Read more]\n\nNote on the Distribution of the Traces of Frobenius\n\nPreprint\n\nJuly 2023\n\n·\n\n16 Reads\n\nN. A. Carella\n\nLet E be a nonsingular elliptic curve over the rational numbers, and let $\\tau^n=p^n+1-\\#E(\\mathbb{F}_{p^n})$. A result in the current literature claims that the normalized traces of Frobenius $\\alpha_n=(\\tau^n+\\overline{\\tau}^n)/2p^{n/2}$ are equidistributed on the interval $[-1,1]$ as $n\\to \\infty$. This short note has two goals: 1. Proposes a correction to this result on the distribution of the traces of Frobenius. 2. Provides a simple elementary proof of this result. More precisely, it is shown that the sequence $\\{\\alpha_n:n\\geq1\\}$ is dense but not equidistributed on the interval $[-1,1]$.\n\n[Button: Read more]\n\nA Result for Germain Primes\n\nPreprint\n\nMarch 2023\n\n·\n\n17 Reads\n\nN. A. Carella\n\nThis article determines a lower bound for the number Germain primes p and 2p+1 up to a large number x.\n\n[Button: Read more]\n\n12345\n\n## Citations (10)\n\n> ... Ahora usamos la ecuación φ 1 (t) para obtener la solución en el intervalo [1,2] . Tenemos que si ...\n> \n> Reference:\n> \n> Hutchinson’s Equation with ﬁnite delay and Prime Numbers Theorem\n\nA Simple Proof Of The Prime Number Theorem\n\nCiting Article\n\nOctober 2015\n\nN. A. Carella\n\n> ... In Carella work [5], an attempt was made to generalize Artin's hypothesis to a set of composite numbers that have a generalized primitive root. At The generalization of Carella [5] for Artin's hypothesis is that, in addition to the set of all primes, the case of the set 2 N is considered, which include composite numbers for which 2  a the primitive root extends to composite numbers, according to the author, has the form:   ,.. . ...\n> \n> Reference:\n> \n> ANALYSIS OF THE STRUCTURES OF ALGEBRAIC DYNAMICAL SYSTEMS BASED ON A COMPUTER SOLUTION OF THE GENERALIZED ARTIN HYPOTHESIS\n\nThe Generalized Artin Primitive Root Conjecture\n\nCiting Article\n> \n> Reference:\n> \n> An Explicit Chebyshev-Type Inequality for Bounding Gaps Between Consecutive Prime Numbers\n\nNote On Prime Gaps And Very Short Intervals\n\nCiting Article\n\nAugust 2010\n\nN. A. Carella\n\n> ... The numbers that do not satisfy (1) are in the set A = {2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, 16, 18, 20, 24, 30, 36, 48, 60, 72, 84, 120, 180, 240, 360, 720, 840, 2520, 5040}. If RH is false, the smallest value of n > 5040 that violates the inequalit